    "ex-qft-four-point-one-loop-log-degree": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "prop-qft-quartic-superficial-degree",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $D\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}$ and let $\\Gamma$ be a finite quartic graph in the sense of [[def-qft-quartic-graph-power-counting]]. With $V$ vertices, $I$ internal edges, $E$ external half-edges, and loop count $L$, the incidence and loop-count identities are $4V=2I+E$ and $L=I-V+1$. Its superficial degree under the stated Euclidean scalar power-counting convention is $\\omega_D(\\Gamma)=DL-2I=D+(D-4)V-\\frac{D-2}{2}E$. In $D=4$ this reduces to $\\omega_4(\\Gamma)=4-E$. A zero value is a logarithmic **superficial** degree by the convention in the definition; these identities do not assert convergence or divergence of an actual graph integral.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Written out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty, there exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for all $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "prop-qft-four-dimensional-log-scale-anomaly",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then $T_\\mu$ is a distribution extending $u$, has scaling degree $4$, and for every $\\mu,\\nu>0$, $$T_\\mu-T_\\nu=\\frac{1}{8\\pi^2}\\log(\\mu/\\nu)\\,\\delta_0.$$ In particular, no extension of $u$ with scaling degree $4$ is exactly homogeneous, where exact homogeneity means $\\rho^4T_\\rho=T$ for every $\\rho>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
